From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2026 18:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 43/43] mungwall: believe the pointer the caller gave, before
 rounding it

The header dump added by the previous patch answered its question on the
first Pi 3 run, and the answer was that nothing was wrong with the block:

    [MungWall] header region at 0x020c87e8:
    [MungWall]   +00: 000000ac 020c8738 020015b0 1adebca1
    [MungWall]   +10: 00000000 00440000 00003662 f46b0204
    [MungWall]   +20: 16243662 5934dbdb dbdbdbdb dbdbdbdb

0x1adebca1 is MUNGWALL_HEADER_ID, and it is at +0x0c, not at +0x08. Read
the region from four bytes higher -- with m68k's two-byte packing, which
puts a 2-byte BOOL at +12 and no padding after it -- and the whole header
parses: magic 0x1adebca1, fault 0, allocsize 68 for an AllocVec of 68,
pool 0, and owner 0x02041624, which is precisely the task doing the free.
The header was never damaged. It was four bytes above where it was looked
for.

MungWall_Check() rounds the pointer down to MEMCHUNK_TOTAL before
computing the header address. That exists for AllocAbs, which can return
an address inside a chunk rather than the chunk's own start, and it is
wrong for everything else the moment the allocator aligns more finely
than MEMCHUNK_TOTAL. On m68k it does: MEMCHUNK_TOTAL is
sizeof(struct MemChunk) = 8, AROS_WORSTALIGN is 4, and TLSF hands out
4-aligned blocks to match. The arithmetic is visible in the message
itself -- it printed 0x020c8830 for a real 0x020c8834, and 72 bytes for a
real 68 plus the 4 it had just added.

The consequence was not cosmetic. The rounded pointer went on to FreeMem,
so TLSF took a block header from inside somebody's wall, called it a
double free and stopped the machine. Every 4-aligned AllocMem on this
architecture was one FreeMem away from that.

So look where the caller pointed first, and only fall back to the
AllocAbs alignment when there is no header there.

This retracts what the previous patch's commit message concluded. The
missing header was not somebody else's write, and this run did not find
ISSUE-0037's writer -- mungwall could not have found anything on m68k in
this state. What it found is why.
--- a/rom/exec/mungwall.c
+++ b/rom/exec/mungwall.c
@@ -179,13 +179,38 @@
 
         D(bug("[MungWall] Freeing %u bytes at 0x%p\n", byteSize, memoryBlock);)
 
-        /* Align size and block to the requirements (needed because of AllocAbs) */
-        byteSize += (IPTR)memoryBlock & (MEMCHUNK_TOTAL - 1);
-        memoryBlock = (APTR)AROS_ROUNDDOWN2((IPTR)memoryBlock, MEMCHUNK_TOTAL);
-
-        /* Take address of mungwall header */
+        /*
+         * Believe the pointer as given, first.
+         *
+         * The rounding below exists for AllocAbs, which can hand back an
+         * address inside a chunk rather than the chunk's own start. It is
+         * wrong for everything else the moment the allocator's alignment is
+         * finer than MEMCHUNK_TOTAL, and on m68k it is: MEMCHUNK_TOTAL is
+         * sizeof(struct MemChunk) = 8, while AROS_WORSTALIGN is 4 and TLSF
+         * hands out 4-aligned blocks accordingly. Rounding a perfectly good
+         * 4-aligned pointer down to 8 moves the header lookup four bytes too
+         * low, so the magic is not where it is looked for and a healthy
+         * allocation is reported as having no header at all -- after which
+         * the rounded pointer goes to FreeMem and TLSF frees a block header
+         * that is really somebody's wall.
+         *
+         * That is not a hypothesis. A Pi 3 run printed the region and the
+         * header was sitting four bytes above, whole: magic 0x1adebca1,
+         * allocsize 68 for an AllocVec of 68, owner exactly the task doing
+         * the free.
+         *
+         * So look where the caller pointed. Only if there is no header there
+         * do the AllocAbs alignment and look again.
+         */
         header = memoryBlock - MUNGWALL_BLOCK_SHIFT;
 
+        if (header->mwh_magicid != MUNGWALL_HEADER_ID)
+        {
+            byteSize += (IPTR)memoryBlock & (MEMCHUNK_TOTAL - 1);
+            memoryBlock = (APTR)AROS_ROUNDDOWN2((IPTR)memoryBlock, MEMCHUNK_TOTAL);
+            header = memoryBlock - MUNGWALL_BLOCK_SHIFT;
+        }
+
         /*
          * A block allocated before mungwall was switched on has no
          * header - what sits below it is whatever the allocator or the
